Morning,

I need help with something that sounds simple but probably is not.

Analytics has been asked to report campaign success, but selected customers m=
ay already have been more likely to convert. The operating teams agree that m=
ore journeys are failing; they disagree on whether the customer, the bank or =
a downstream party owns the failure. Different teams have produced different =
answers from the same period, so population and timing rules need to be visib=
le.

The questions I would ask in the room are:
1. What would we expect to see if service problems suppress otherwise suitabl=
e offers?
2. What evidence would instead support that targeting explains the apparent l=
ift?
3. How do we rule out that the campaign creates incremental response?

This has returned because the previous answer was useful but not strong enoug=
h to become a standing rule. Use February 2022 as the new evidence point rath=
er than recycling the earlier conclusion. Please work towards the treatment, =
control, success window and guardrail measures before the next campaign launc=
hes. A useful output would be a model-ready table, data dictionary and an hon=
est baseline.

Use campaign design and customer responses; monthly account snapshots, status=
 events, limits, product enrolments and signatories; transaction-level timest=
amps, channels, amounts, statuses and error context; customer contacts, compl=
aints and suggestions. One caution: Historical campaign responses alone do no=
t identify causal impact where no comparable control group exists.

